KG42.register("source_edge_archive-b02e5880bcf7d381208fd686",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::E::v141-c-R004-E002","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E002","source":"bidding.supply","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-b3f649e90c1f17b9","target":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::V::bidding.supply","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.price_signal","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::bidding.supply","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. | Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. | Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E14"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"3646a46e51492db2a2dbe9712a26c19920654a34841770425d6a71edc4087f63","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","target_definition":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^{k}-\\rho\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)/2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e18"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. | Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. | Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E14"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E002","source":"bidding.supply","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}});
